# nailong-cli

An interactive command line for `nailong-agent-sdk`: a coding agent in your terminal with
approvals before it writes or runs anything, permission modes including a read-only plan mode,
optional sandboxing, saved and forkable sessions, `@file` mentions, your own commands and skills,
hooks, MCP servers, subagents, plugins, images, background commands, extra directories, a
worktree per session, `/compact`, an undo for what it changes, and reviewed creation of its own
tools. There is no login or account: you bring an API key and everything runs locally.

**New here? Read [`docs/cli-guide.md`](../../docs/cli-guide.md), a task-by-task guide.**

It implements the baseline that Claude Code and Codex share; see
[`docs/cli-parity.md`](../../docs/cli-parity.md) for what is built, partly built and not built, and
[`docs/interactive-cli.md`](../../docs/interactive-cli.md) for the design. It is tested end to end
with scripted models and **has not been run against a live model yet**.

```bash
cd apps/nailong_cli
uv sync
uv run nailong --help
```

## Configure

Create `.nailong/config.toml` in your project (or `~/.config/nailong/config.toml` for every
project; flags override the project file, which overrides the user file):

```toml
provider = "anthropic"            # or "openai-compatible"
model = "your-model-name"
api_key_env = "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY" # the NAME of the environment variable; the key never goes in a file

# optional
permission_mode = "default"       # default | accept-edits | plan | bypass
max_steps = 12
add_dirs = ["/abs/path/to/another/project"]   # more directories the agent may use
sandbox = "auto"                  # auto | none | docker
docker_image = "python:3.12-slim"
web = false                       # let the agent fetch public web pages (asks each time)
# base_url = "openrouter"         # openai-compatible: a URL, or one of openai, openrouter, gemini, xai

[prices.your-model-name]          # lets /cost and --max-budget-usd count spending
input_per_million = 3.0
cached_input_per_million = 0.3
output_per_million = 15.0

[agents.reviewer]                 # a read-only subagent the main agent can call
description = "Reviews a change for bugs."
instructions = "You review code. Read the files you are given and report problems."
```

Settings that run programs, `[[hooks]]` and `[mcp.NAME]`, are read **only from your user file**;
in a project file they are ignored and `/doctor` says so.

```toml
# ~/.config/nailong/config.toml
[[hooks]]
event = "pre_tool"                # post_tool, session_start, session_end, prompt_submit, turn_end
tool = "run_command"              # a glob; default *
command = "scripts/check-command.sh"   # exit non-zero to refuse; gets NAILONG_TOOL, NAILONG_TOOL_INPUT

[mcp.files]
command = "npx"
args = ["-y", "some-mcp-server"]
```

Put project instructions in `NAILONG.md`, `AGENTS.md` or `CLAUDE.md`; your own are in
`~/.config/nailong/NAILONG.md`. Custom commands are `.nailong/commands/NAME.md` (with
`$ARGUMENTS`), skills are `.nailong/skills/NAME.md` (the agent sees a one-line index and loads a
skill with `use_skill` when it matches). Plugins are directories in `~/.config/nailong/plugins/` or
`--plugin-dir`.

## Your own model, on your own network

For confidential work, point the CLI at a server you host (vLLM, llama.cpp, Ollama, LM Studio):

```toml
# ~/.config/nailong/config.toml
provider = "openai-compatible"
model = "your-served-model-name"
base_url = "vllm"                  # or ollama | lmstudio | llamacpp | http://gpu-box:8000/v1
allow_insecure_http = true         # only needed for plain HTTP beyond this machine
timeout_seconds = 300
offline = true                     # refuse any setting that reaches beyond your network
```

No key is needed unless you set `api_key_env`. `offline` (or `--offline`) is read only from your
user file, so a project cannot switch it off. See [`docs/local-models.md`](../../docs/local-models.md)
for what the server must offer and what `offline` does and does not guarantee.
`invalid_output_retries` (default 2) is how many times in a row the model is asked again after an
unusable reply.

## Use

```bash
nailong                           # interactive
nailong "explain this project"    # interactive, starting with a message
nailong -c                        # resume the latest session  (-r ID for a named one)
nailong -c --fork-session         # branch it instead
nailong -p "fix the failing test" # run once and exit; piped input is added to the prompt
nailong -p "..." --output json    # status, answer, steps, cost, session as one JSON object
nailong -p "..." --output stream-json   # one JSON event per line while it runs
nailong --permission-mode plan    # read-only: investigate and propose a plan
nailong --allow "run_command:pytest*" --disallow "run_command:rm *"
nailong --mcp-config servers.json --web --search --forge
nailong --image error.png "what does this error mean?"
nailong --add-dir ../shared-lib    # another directory the agent can read, edit and run in
nailong --worktree fix-login       # work in a separate git worktree
nailong --effort high --max-budget-usd 2 --autocompact
nailong --review                   # read-only review of your uncommitted changes, then exit
nailong -n "login bug"             # name the session; /search and /export find it later
```

Exit codes: 0 completed, 1 failed, 2 blocked, 64 setup problem, 130 interrupted.

Inside, type a message, or:

| | |
|---|---|
| `@path` | attach a file or directory listing to the message |
| `!command` | run a command yourself |
| `/mode` `/permissions` | permission mode; saved rules (`allow tool:pattern`, `remove N`) |
| `/model` `/status` `/doctor` `/context` `/compact` `/cost` | settings and what is sent each turn |
| `/sessions` `/resume` `/fork` `/new` (`/clear`) | sessions |
| `/init` `/review` `/diff` `/rewind` | draft `AGENTS.md`, review or show your changes, undo what the agent changed |
| `/export` `/search` `/suggest` | session as markdown, find text in sessions, three next prompts |
| `/add-dir` `/jobs` `/kill` `/plugins` | more directories, background commands, plugins |
| `/memory` `/skills` `/agents` `/mcp` `/todos` `/tools` `/sandbox` `/verify` `/forge` | what the agent has |

## How it keeps you in control

- **Reading is free; writing and commands ask.** You see the diff or the command line first.
  Answers: yes once, for this session, always (saved), or no. A "no" goes back to the model as a
  failed tool call, so it can try something else.
- **Plan mode** removes the write and command tools. After the plan you choose to carry it out
  accepting edits, carry it out asking each time, or stay in plan mode.
- **Saved rules live outside the project**, in `~/.config/nailong/permissions.toml`, one set per
  project path. The project's `.nailong/` directory and `NAILONG.md` cannot be written by the
  agent's file tools.
- **Sandboxing.** `sandbox = "auto"` uses bubblewrap on Linux or Seatbelt on macOS when present.
  Otherwise commands are **not sandboxed**, the prompt says so in red, they are never allowed for a
  whole session, and unattended runs refuse them unless you pass `--allow-unsandboxed`.
  `bypass` mode needs a sandbox or that flag. On Windows use `sandbox = "docker"`.
- **No terminal means no guessing.** In `-p` mode an action that needs approval is refused unless
  a rule covers it.
- **Ctrl+C** cancels the current turn; a second press within two seconds exits.
- **In a terminal** the prompt sits between rules with a hint bar; `/` opens a command menu, `@`
  completes file names, Shift+Tab cycles default, accept-edits, auto and plan, and a backslash then
  Enter (or Alt+Enter) starts a new line. Esc stops a turn, approvals are an arrow-key menu, answers render as markdown, tool output is collapsed (Ctrl+O or `/expand`).

## Known limits

- Not yet run against a live model. The model's replies are JSON turns, so the live view extracts
  the `answer` text as it streams; with a custom `--json-schema` it prints at the end instead.
- bubblewrap and Seatbelt are tested on the command lines they build, not run; background jobs,
  worktrees and the prompt_toolkit prompt were not exercised in a real terminal here.
- A shell command can still edit `.nailong/config.toml` unless a sandbox keeps it read-only.
  Saved rules and hooks are outside the project, so such an edit cannot grant itself permission.
- Deliberately absent: OAuth or any account, IDE integration, desktop, remote or cloud sessions,
  a plugin marketplace, `/cd`. The parity document lists the rest.

